<Style id="hotel"><IconStyle><color>ff2a2a2a</color><scale>1.2</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/wht-stars.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
<Folder><name>Seoul · 57 Myeongdong Hostel (28–31 Oct)</name>
<Placemark><name>Hotel: 57 Myeongdong Hostel</name><styleUrl>#hotel</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>28–31 Oct</b><br>A small, well-rated hostel in the middle of Myeongdong — a short walk from the Namsan cable car and the evening street-food stalls.<br>Check-in from 15:00 · out 11:00]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9834441,37.5609967,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 28 Oct 15:00 · Check in at 57 Myeongdong Hostel</name><styleUrl>#Seoul</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[A small, well-rated hostel in the middle of Myeongdong — a short walk from the Namsan cable car and the evening street-food stalls.<br><b>Hours:</b> Check-in from 15:00 · out 11:00<br>• Arriving early? Leave the bags at reception.<br>• Buy a T-money card (₩4,000 + top-up) at the first convenience store you see.<br>• Nearest metro: Myeongdong (Line 4) exit 8.<br>Naver: 57명동호스텔]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9834441,37.5609967,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 28 Oct 16:30 · Namsan Cable Car (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Up Namsan<br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩15,000 return · ₩12,000 one way<br>A 605 m aerial tramway running since 1962 that lifts you from the edge of Myeongdong to the top of Namsan in about three minutes.<br><b>Hours:</b> 10:00–23:00<br>• Be in line by 16:30 — the queue balloons before sunset.<br>• Stand on the right side going up for the city view.<br>• Buy a return: the walk down is 30 min in the dark.<br>Naver: 남산케이블카]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9839744,37.5565908,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 28 Oct 16:30 · Walk up Namsan via the Oreumi elevator (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Up Namsan<br><b>Walk · 35–40 min</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>Take the free glass "Oreumi" incline elevator from Hoehyeon to the foot of the mountain, then the stepped Baekbeom Square path up through the woods to the tower plaza.<br><i>Free, no queue, and the city opens up behind you with every flight of stairs.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> Elevator 09:00–23:00<br>• Comfortable shoes; the stairs are steady but long.<br>• Take the cable car down if the legs are done (₩12,000 one way).<br>Naver: 남산 오르미]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9813,37.5587,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 28 Oct 17:20 · N Seoul Tower &amp; love locks (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Namsan at sunset<br><b>Price:</b> Terrace free · observatory ≈₩21,000<br>Seoul's landmark broadcast tower on the 243 m Namsan peak. The open terrace and the fences of love locks are free; the observatory sits 236 m above the mountain.<br><b>Hours:</b> 10:30–22:30 (Fri–Sat to 23:00)<br>• Bring a padlock from a stationery shop (≈₩3,000); on the terrace they cost more.<br>• Be up by 17:00 for the blue hour, stay until the city lights come on.<br>• The tower's colour shows the air quality — blue means clean.<br>Naver: N서울타워]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9882266,37.5511694,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 28 Oct 17:20 · Palgakjeong pavilion &amp; the beacon mounds (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Namsan at sunset<br><b>Viewpoint</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>Skip the paid observatory: the octagonal Palgakjeong pavilion and the Joseon-era beacon mounds share the summit plaza and the same sunset view over the city.<br><i>The same 360° sunset for ₩0, with the tower lighting up right above you.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> Always open<br>• Beacon-lighting ceremony most days at 11:30 and 15:00.<br>• The best light is 15 min after sunset, when the tower turns colour.<br>Naver: 남산 봉수대]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9882,37.5512,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 28 Oct 19:30 · Namsan Donkatsu 101 (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Dinner after the tower<br><b>Korean · giant pork cutlet</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩13–16k per person, food only<br>Namsan's famous wang-donkkaseu — a plate-sized, thin, crisp cutlet in sweet brown sauce — right below the cable-car station. Seoul taxi drivers have eaten here for 30 years.<br><i>The original of the Namsan cutlet houses; huge portions, pickled radish refills.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> No reservations; short queue on weekends.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈10:30–21:30<br>• One cutlet each is more than enough; share a side of udon.<br>• Card accepted.<br>Naver: 101번지남산돈까스]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9829,37.5571,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 28 Oct 19:30 · Myeongdong Kyoja (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Dinner after the tower<br><b>Korean · kalguksu noodle soup &amp; mandu</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩11–16k per person, food only<br>Knife-cut noodles in a rich chicken broth and plump dumplings — a Michelin Bib Gourmand institution since 1966, famous for its fiercely garlicky kimchi.<br><i>Only four dishes on the menu; the kimchi is legendary.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> No reservations, fast turnover — 10–20 min queue at peak.<br><b>Hours:</b> 10:30–21:00<br>• Kalguksu ₩11k, mandu ₩13k — split both.<br>• Two branches 100 m apart; the smaller one has shorter lines.<br>Naver: 명동교자 본점]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9857,37.5627,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 28 Oct 19:30 · Myeongdong street food (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Dinner after the tower<br><b>Street food · grazing</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩10–15k per person, food only<br>Korea's busiest shopping street turns into an open-air food market at night. Graze from stall to stall.<br><i>Tornado potato, tteokbokki, egg bread (gyeranppang), hotteok, grilled cheese lobster if you're feeling flush.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None — just walk.<br><b>Hours:</b> Stalls ≈16:00–23:00<br>• Skip the ₩15k lobster tails if you're watching the budget.<br>• Cards work at most stalls; cash is quicker.<br>Naver: 명동거리]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9853,37.5636,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 28 Oct 19:30 · Euljiro Nogari Alley (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Dinner after the tower<br><b>Korean pub food · dried pollack &amp; draught beer</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩10–15k per person, food only<br>An alley of 1980s beer halls where the whole street sits at plastic tables over grilled dried pollack (nogari, ₩1–2k each) and cheap draught beer — 'Hip-jiro' at its most retro.<br><i>Manseon Hof is the original; the street itself is a registered 'Seoul Future Heritage'.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; grab any free table.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈15:00–24:00 · quieter on Sundays<br>• Order nogari + a plate of fried chicken gizzards or golbaengi to share.<br>• Drinks are extra: draught ≈₩4k.<br>Naver: 을지로 노가리골목]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9915,37.5665,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 29 Oct 09:30 · Gyeongbokgung Palace (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Morning in Gwanghwamun<br><b>Price:</b> ₩3,000<br>The main royal palace of the Joseon dynasty (1395), restored after its demolition under Japanese rule. Grand halls, pavilion ponds and a mountain backdrop — the postcard image of Seoul.<br><b>Hours:</b> 09:00–18:00 (Sep–Oct) · last entry 1 h before · <b>Closed:</b> Tuesdays<br>• Royal guard changing at 10:00 at Gwanghwamun gate — arrive by 09:50.<br>• Free English tours at 11:00 and 13:30 from the info centre.<br>• Photo spots: Gyeonghoeru pavilion pond, Hyangwonjeong.<br>Naver: 경복궁]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.977041,37.579617,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 29 Oct 09:30 · Gwanghwamun Square &amp; Cheonggyecheon stream (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Morning in Gwanghwamun<br><b>Walk</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>The grand axis in front of the palace: King Sejong and Admiral Yi statues, the fountain lawn, and a few minutes east the sunken Cheonggyecheon stream with its stepping stones.<br><i>The postcard view of Gwanghwamun gate with Bugaksan behind — no ticket needed.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> Always open<br>• Still catch the 10:00 guard change at the gate from the square.<br>• Cheonggyecheon stepping stones at Gwangtonggyo are the photo spot.<br>Naver: 광화문광장]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9769,37.5759,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 29 Oct 11:15 · National Folk Museum of Korea (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> A museum by the palace<br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>Inside the palace grounds: three galleries on how Koreans lived, worked and celebrated for the last 600 years, plus an open-air street of 1970s shopfronts.<br><b>Hours:</b> 09:00–18:00<br>• Enter from inside Gyeongbokgung — no extra ticket.<br>• The outdoor 'Street of Memories' is a fun quick photo stop.<br>Naver: 국립민속박물관]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9785,37.5815,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 29 Oct 11:15 · National Palace Museum of Korea (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> A museum by the palace<br><b>Museum</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>Royal treasures of the Joseon court — thrones, seals, hanbok, the astronomical clock — in a quiet modern building by the palace's west gate.<br><i>Fewer crowds than the Folk Museum and the gold-leaf royal robes are stunning.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> 10:00–18:00 (Wed/Sat to 21:00)<br>• Enter from Gwanghwamun exit 2 side; no palace ticket needed.<br>• The basement gallery of royal carriages is the sleeper hit.<br>Naver: 국립고궁박물관]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.975,37.5765,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 29 Oct 12:30 · Tosokchon Samgyetang (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Lunch by the palace<br><b>Korean · ginseng chicken soup</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩20–25k per person, food only<br>A whole young chicken stuffed with rice, ginseng, jujube and garlic, simmered in a hanok courtyard restaurant that presidents have eaten in.<br><i>The most famous samgyetang in Seoul; comes with a shot of ginseng liquor.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> No reservations; 15–30 min queue at lunch — arrive by 12:15.<br><b>Hours:</b> 10:00–22:00<br>• One bowl each (₩20k); the black-chicken version is ₩5k more.<br>• Gyeongbokgung station exit 2, 5 min west of the palace.<br>Naver: 토속촌삼계탕]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9713,37.577,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 29 Oct 12:30 · Samcheongdong Sujebi (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Lunch by the palace<br><b>Korean · hand-torn dough soup</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩12–16k per person, food only<br>A 40-year institution on the Samcheong-dong lane: chewy hand-torn dough in an anchovy-clam broth, with a giant kimchi pancake to share.<br><i>Cash-only feel, tiny tables, exactly two things worth ordering.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; short queue 12:30–13:30.<br><b>Hours:</b> 11:00–20:30<br>• Sujebi ≈₩9k each + one kimchi-jeon (₩12k) between you.<br>• 10 min walk north from the museum along the palace wall.<br>Naver: 삼청동수제비]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9812,37.585,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 29 Oct 14:00 · Pottery-painting class in Bukchon (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Afternoon in Bukchon<br><b>Workshop · 1.5–2 h</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩30–45k per person<br>Paint your own cups or plates in a small hanok studio; pieces are glazed and fired and can be collected in a few days or shipped.<br><i>You leave with matching cups you made together.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> Book a 14:00 slot a few days ahead via Naver or Klook; several studios sit around Bukchon-ro and Gye-dong.<br><b>Hours:</b> Slots ≈11:00–18:00<br>• Ask about pick-up before Nov 6, or shipping to your last hotel.<br>• Wear nothing precious — glaze stains.<br>Naver: 북촌 도자기 페인팅]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9862,37.5812,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 29 Oct 14:00 · Wander the Bukchon lanes (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Afternoon in Bukchon<br><b>Stroll</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>A 600-year-old neighbourhood of hanok (traditional wooden houses) on the hill between the two palaces, still home to Seoulites.<br><i>Bukchon-ro 11-gil: tiled roofs with the skyline behind them.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> Visitor hours 10:00–17:00 · residential lanes closed Sundays<br>• People live here: quiet voices, no music.<br>• Fines apply in restricted lanes outside visitor hours.<br>Naver: 북촌한옥마을]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9849519,37.5814696,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 29 Oct 16:00 · Insadong tea house &amp; Ssamziegil (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Tea or temple<br><b>Tea &amp; crafts</b><br><b>Price:</b> Tea ≈₩8–12k · Ssamziegil free<br>Seoul's traditional arts street: antique shops, galleries, teahouses and the spiralling Ssamziegil craft arcade.<br><i>Order omija (five-flavour berry) or yuja tea in a traditional teahouse.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> Ssamziegil 10:30–20:30<br>• Good gifts: hanji paper, ceramics, tea.<br>• Shin Old Tea House hides in an alley off the main street.<br>Naver: 인사동 쌈지길]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9849036,37.5743024,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 29 Oct 16:00 · Jogyesa Temple (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Tea or temple<br><b>Temple</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>Head temple of Korean Buddhism's Jogye Order, hidden behind Insadong: a 500-year-old white pine, a main hall with three huge golden Buddhas, and thousands of hanging lanterns.<br><i>Chrysanthemum festival displays usually fill the courtyard in late October / early November.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; walk in.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈04:00–21:00<br>• Take your shoes off to sit inside the hall for a few minutes.<br>• Two minutes from Insadong's main street.<br>Naver: 조계사]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9818,37.574,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 29 Oct 18:00 · Koong — Gaeseong dumplings (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Early dinner<br><b>Korean · North-Korean-style dumplings</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩13–18k per person, food only<br>Fist-sized Gaeseong-style dumplings, dumpling soup and pyeonsu (square summer dumplings) from a family recipe that's been served in Insadong for over 40 years.<br><i>Michelin Bib Gourmand; the dumplings are hand-pleated in the window.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None needed at 18:00.<br><b>Hours:</b> 11:30–21:00 · last orders 20:30<br>• Mandu-guk (₩14k) + a plate of steamed mandu (₩15k) to share.<br>• Five minutes from Ssamziegil and Jogyesa.<br>Naver: 개성만두 궁 인사동본점]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9861,37.5734,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 29 Oct 18:00 · Woo Lae Oak — cold noodles only (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Early dinner<br><b>Korean · Pyongyang naengmyeon</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩17–20k per person, food only<br>Seoul's most storied restaurant (1946): delicate Pyongyang-style buckwheat noodles in a clear, cold beef broth, served in a grand old dining room.<br><i>The naengmyeon that Seoul food critics measure all others by.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> No reservations; queue at 18:00 is short. Closed Mondays.<br><b>Hours:</b> 11:30–21:30 · <b>Closed:</b> Mondays<br>• Budget tip: order naengmyeon only (≈₩17k) — the bulgogi is ₩50k+.<br>• Euljiro 4-ga station exit 4; taxi 7 min from Insadong.<br>Naver: 우래옥 본점]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.998,37.5672,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 29 Oct 19:30 · Moonlight Lotus Show at Changgyeonggung (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Evening event<br><b>Night event · reserved entry</b><br><b>Price:</b> Reserved entry · ≈₩1,000–3,000<br>An evening of light on the palace's lotus pond (Chundangji) and the Victorian glasshouse — reserved-entry sessions with the whole palace lit after dark.<br><i>The lotus pond and the 1909 glasshouse lit from the water; almost silent.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> Reserved sessions 19:00 / 19:30 / 20:00 — foreigners book via Creatrip.<br><b>Hours:</b> Sessions 19:00 / 19:30 / 20:00<br>• Foreigners book via Creatrip — the Korean sites need a Korean phone number.<br>• Enter through Honghwamun, the main gate on Changgyeonggung-ro.<br>• Taxi back to Myeongdong ≈15 min, ≈₩9k.<br>Naver: 창경궁]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.995,37.5788,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 29 Oct 19:30 · Cheonggyecheon by night &amp; Gwanghwamun (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Evening event<br><b>Night walk</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>The sunken stream lit after dark from Cheonggye Plaza east, then up to Gwanghwamun Square with the palace gate and the mountains floodlit behind it.<br><i>Candle-lit waterfall at the plaza; the lantern festival sometimes runs here in early November.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h · lights until ≈23:00<br>• Start at Cheonggye Plaza (Line 5 Gwanghwamun exit 5) and walk 20 min east, then back.<br>• Ten minutes on foot to Myeongdong afterwards.<br>Naver: 청계광장]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.978,37.569,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 29 Oct 19:30 · Jongno 3-ga pocha street (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Evening event<br><b>Street tents · drinks &amp; snacks</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩15–25k per person, food &amp; drinks<br>After 19:00 the lane behind Jongno 3-ga station fills with orange tents and plastic stools: grilled clams, eomuk, soju and the whole city's office crowd.<br><i>The most Seoul night out there is, for the price of two drinks.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None — sit wherever there's a stool.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈18:00–02:00<br>• Order one dish per tent and move along.<br>• Line 1/3/5 Jongno 3-ga, exit 6.<br>Naver: 종로3가 포장마차 거리]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9918,37.5712,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 30 Oct 10:30 · Hadongkwan — gomtang (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Brunch<br><b>Korean · clear beef soup</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩18k per person, food only<br>Since 1939: a bowl of clear beef-and-tripe soup over rice, seasoned at the table with salt and spring onion. Seoul's classic morning-after breakfast.<br><i>They close when the broth runs out — often by mid-afternoon.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; walk in before 12:00.<br><b>Hours:</b> 07:00–16:00 · <b>Closed:</b> Sundays<br>• 'Botong' (regular, ≈₩18k) is plenty; add kimchi juice to the broth like the regulars.<br>• Two minutes from the hostel.<br>Naver: 하동관 명동점]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9828,37.5638,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 30 Oct 10:30 · Myeongdong Kyoja (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Brunch<br><b>Korean · kalguksu noodle soup &amp; mandu</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩11–16k per person, food only<br>Knife-cut noodles in chicken broth and plump dumplings at the 1966 Bib Gourmand institution — quiet and quick at 10:30.<br><i>The garlic kimchi is legendary.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 10:30–21:00<br>• Kalguksu ₩11k, mandu ₩13k — split both.<br>Naver: 명동교자 본점]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9857,37.5627,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 30 Oct 11:45 · Namsangol Hanok Village (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Hanok or shopping<br><b>Heritage</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>Five restored aristocratic hanok moved here from around Seoul, set in a traditional garden at the foot of Namsan with a time capsule buried for the year 2394.<br><i>Quiet courtyards, free traditional-games corner, autumn colour in the garden.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 09:00–21:00 · closed Mondays<br>• Chungmuro station exit 3–4.<br>• Free tea ceremony demos some afternoons.<br>Naver: 남산골한옥마을]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9943,37.5594,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 30 Oct 11:45 · Myeongdong shopping &amp; K-beauty (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Hanok or shopping<br><b>Shopping</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free to wander<br>Olive Young's flagship, Innisfree, Etude and every K-beauty brand within four blocks — quieter before noon.<br><i>Tax refund at the counter for purchases over ₩15k with your passport.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> Shops ≈10:30–22:00<br>• Sheet masks make light gifts.<br>• Skip the tourist-trap cosmetics touts on the main drag.<br>Naver: 명동거리]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9853,37.5636,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 30 Oct 13:30 · Gyeonghuigung Palace &amp; Seoul Museum of History (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Quiet palace or sky garden<br><b>Palace · free</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>The least-visited of Seoul's five palaces: a hillside of quiet halls behind the Seoul Museum of History, often almost empty on a weekday afternoon.<br><i>A palace with no queue and no crowds — and the city museum next door has a huge scale model of all of Seoul.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> 09:00–18:00 · last entry 17:30 · <b>Closed:</b> Mondays<br>• Enter through Heunghwamun gate; the main hall Sungjeongjeon sits up the slope with a view back over the roofs.<br>• Seoul Museum of History (free, 09:00–20:00) is in the same grounds — the 1:1500 model of the city is worth 20 minutes.<br>• Gwanghwamun and the Cheonggyecheon spring are a 10-minute walk downhill.<br>Naver: 경희궁]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9683,37.5712,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 30 Oct 13:30 · Seoullo 7017 sky garden (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Quiet palace or sky garden<br><b>Elevated garden walk · free</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>A 1970s motorway overpass turned into a kilometre-long garden floating above Seoul Station — 24,000 plants in giant pots, little cafés, foot baths and a view over the old station roof.<br><i>A garden in the sky over the trains; at dusk the whole walkway lights up blue.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> Open 24 h<br>• Start at the Namdaemun Market end and walk west — the sunset is over the station roof.<br>• Namdaemun's hotteok and kalguksu alley is right below the east end.<br>• Warm foot bath near the Malli-dong end (free, bring a small towel).<br>Naver: 서울로 7017]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9705,37.5567,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 30 Oct 16:00 · Yeouido Hangang Park (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Han River afternoon<br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>The busiest stretch of the Han River park, on the island that holds the National Assembly — grass, bike paths and the riverside promenade.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h<br>• Tandem bikes rent for ≈₩10,000/h.<br>• The cruise pier is a 5-min walk from the station.<br>Naver: 여의도한강공원]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9347112,37.5267106,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 30 Oct 16:00 · Seonyudo Park — island garden (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Han River afternoon<br><b>Park</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>A former water-treatment plant turned garden island in the Han: ivy over old concrete tanks, a time garden, an arched footbridge, river views both ways.<br><i>The most romantic park in Seoul — moody, green and strangely quiet.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> 06:00–24:00<br>• Enter over the Seonyugyo footbridge from Yanghwa Hangang Park.<br>• From here the cruise pier is a 10-min taxi — allow for it.<br>Naver: 선유도공원]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.8995,37.5436,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 30 Oct 17:00 · Sunset Han River cruise (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Sunset on the river<br><b>River cruise · 40–70 min</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩17–30k per person by cruise type<br>Cruises leave the Yeouido pier and loop under the bridges — at sunset you get the 63 Building, Namsan and the bridge lights all at once.<br><i>The 63 Building, Namsan and the bridge lights from the water.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> Book the sailing closest to the 17:38 sunset a week ahead.<br><b>Hours:</b> Sunset sailing ≈16:40–17:40<br>• Book the sailing closest to the 17:38 sunset.<br>• The open upper deck is cold in November — bring a layer.<br>Naver: 이랜드크루즈 여의도]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9322,37.5289,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 30 Oct 17:00 · Banpo Bridge Moonlight Rainbow Fountain (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Sunset on the river<br><b>Night show</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>The longest bridge fountain in the world: 380 jets along both sides of Banpo Bridge, lit in colour and set to music, watched from the grass of Banpo Hangang Park.<br><i>Late October is the very end of the season — the last shows of the year.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None. Evening shows usually 20:00, 20:30, 21:00 (20 min each); check the Hangang park site for the day.<br><b>Hours:</b> Shows ≈20:00–21:00 (Apr–Oct)<br>• Taxi ≈15 min from Yeouido (≈₩10k) or Line 9 to Sinbanggyo.<br>• Eat first on Yeouido — the fountain is a show, not a dinner spot.<br>Naver: 반포대교 달빛무지개분수]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9966,37.5125,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 30 Oct 19:00 · River ramyeon at the convenience store (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Dinner by the river<br><b>Instant noodles · Seoul classic</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩5–8k per person, food only<br>Cooking instant noodles at a Han River convenience store is a Seoul rite of passage — cheap, warm and strangely romantic.<br><i>Self-cook ramyeon machines, riverside tables, bridge lights.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None — 24 h.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h<br>• Add a triangle kimbap and a can of Cass — that's the date.<br>• Taxi back to Myeongdong ≈20 min, ≈₩15k.<br>Naver: 여의도한강공원 편의점]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9362,37.5272,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 30 Oct 19:00 · The Hyundai Seoul — B1 food hall (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Dinner by the river<br><b>Food hall · everything</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩12–18k per person, food only<br>Seoul's most beautiful department store: an indoor forest under a glass roof, and a basement food hall of Seoul's trendiest bakeries, ramen, gimbap and dessert counters.<br><i>Sounds Forest on the 5th floor is worth the escalator ride before you eat.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; food hall open till 20:30 on Fridays.<br><b>Hours:</b> 10:30–20:30 (Fri)<br>• Five minutes' walk west from Yeouinaru; pick two counters and share.<br>• Taxi home ≈20 min, or Line 5 → Line 2 back to Euljiro.<br>Naver: 더현대 서울]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9285,37.5259,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Seoul · Haru Forest Hotel Seongsu &amp; Konkuk (31 Oct – 2 Nov)</name>
<Placemark><name>Hotel: Haru Forest Hotel Seongsu &amp; Konkuk</name><styleUrl>#hotel</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>31 Oct – 2 Nov</b><br>Your base for two nights in Seongsu–Konkuk (광진구 화양동 23-21), about 5 min on foot from Konkuk Univ. station — the east-side neighbourhood of converted factories, bakeries and the Common Ground container mall.<br>Korean listings show overnight check-in 20:00 · checkout 13:00<br><i>Confirm the check-in time with the hotel and ask to drop bags at 11:30.</i>]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0697,37.5405,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sat 31 Oct 11:00 · Check out, drop bags at Haru Forest</name><styleUrl>#Seoul</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[Your base for two nights in Seongsu–Konkuk (광진구 화양동 23-21), about 5 min on foot from Konkuk Univ. station — the east-side neighbourhood of converted factories, bakeries and the Common Ground container mall.<br><b>Hours:</b> Korean listings show overnight check-in 20:00 · checkout 13:00<br>• Korean listings show overnight check-in 20:00 / checkout 13:00 — confirm check-in and drop the bags at 11:30.<br>• Konkuk Univ. station (Lines 2 &amp; 7) is 5 min from the hotel.<br>Naver: 하루포레스트호텔 성수&amp;건대점]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0697,37.5405,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sat 31 Oct 12:30 · Seongsu Jokbal (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Lunch in Seongsu<br><b>Korean · braised pig's trotters</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩20–23k per person, food only<br>Seongsu's landmark for 40 years: soy-braised trotters sliced thin, wrapped in lettuce with garlic and salted shrimp sauce.<br><i>Order the 'half-half' — original and spicy — with a bowl of cold bean-sprout soup.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; lunch queues are short.<br><b>Hours:</b> 11:30–22:00<br>• A medium (≈₩40k) feeds two comfortably.<br>• Seongsu station exit 4.<br>Naver: 성수족발]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0556,37.5449,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sat 31 Oct 12:30 · Cafe Onion Seongsu (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Lunch in Seongsu<br><b>Bakery-café · pastries &amp; brunch</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩10–14k per person, food only<br>A bombed-out 1970s factory turned into Seoul's most photographed bakery: raw concrete, an open courtyard and trays of pandoro dusted with sugar.<br><i>The pandoro 'snow mountain' pastry and the rooftop.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; weekends queue at the counter.<br><b>Hours:</b> 08:00–22:00 (Sat–Sun 10:00–)<br>• Two pastries + two coffees ≈₩25k total.<br>• Seongsu station exit 2, 3 min.<br>Naver: 카페 어니언 성수]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0575,37.5445,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sat 31 Oct 14:30 · Perfume-making workshop (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Make something or ride<br><b>Workshop · 1.5 h</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩40–60k per person<br>Blend your own 50 ml fragrance from 40-odd notes with a perfumer's guidance in one of the studios around Seongsu station — you each leave with a bottle named by the other.<br><i>A very Seoul date; the bottles make the best souvenir.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> Book a 14:30 Saturday slot 1–2 weeks ahead via Naver or Klook.<br><b>Hours:</b> Slots ≈11:00–19:00<br>• Most studios speak enough English; Klook listings are in English.<br>• Bottles are liquid — pack them in checked luggage.<br>Naver: 성수 향수 공방]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0548,37.544,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sat 31 Oct 14:30 · Han River bike ride on Seoul Bike (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Make something or ride<br><b>Active · 1.5 h</b><br><b>Price:</b> ₩1,000 per hour (Ttareungyi app)<br>Rent the green city bikes (Ttareungyi) and ride the riverside path from Ttukseom to Jamsil Bridge and back — flat, car-free and golden in late October.<br><i>The path passes under Cheongdam Bridge with the Lotte Tower ahead.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; register in the Seoul Bike app with a foreign card.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h<br>• Return to any dock; 1-hour passes can be renewed.<br>• Helmets are not provided — ride slowly.<br>Naver: 따릉이 뚝섬유원지]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0665,37.531,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sat 31 Oct 16:30 · Seoul Forest (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Seongsu afternoon<br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>A 1.2 km² park on a former racecourse — wetlands, a deer enclosure and, at the end of October, an avenue of gold ginkgo.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h<br>• Deer park and the ginkgo path are near the main gate.<br>• A footbridge over the tracks leads on to the river park.<br>Naver: 서울숲]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0374424,37.5443878,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sat 31 Oct 16:30 · Seongsu café &amp; design streets (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Seongsu afternoon<br><b>Stroll · cafés · shops</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free (coffee ≈₩6–8k)<br>Brooklyn-of-Seoul: red-brick factories turned cafés, sneaker flagships, pop-up stores and small design shops along Yeonmujang-gil and Seongsui-ro.<br><i>Pop-ups change weekly — you never see the same street twice.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> Shops ≈11:00–21:00<br>• Daelim Changgo and Onion are the two classic warehouse cafés.<br>• Good gift shopping: Point of View (stationery), Ader Error.<br>Naver: 성수동 카페거리]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.056,37.5445,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sat 31 Oct 18:00 · Picnic at Ttukseom: chicken &amp; beer (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Picnic at the river<br><b>Picnic · fried chicken &amp; beer</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩12–15k per person, food only<br>Riverside park with delivery zones and picnic mats for rent — where Seoulites do chimaek (chicken and beer) on the grass. Tonight the sky show is here too.<br><b>Hours:</b> Delivery to the park until ≈23:00<br>• Order from a delivery kiosk or app and give them your pillar number.<br>• Halloween night: avoid Itaewon and Hongdae — the river is the right place to be.<br>• Mats rent for ≈₩5k; bring a layer, it's cold by the water.<br>Naver: 뚝섬한강공원]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0699562,37.5293507,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sat 31 Oct 18:00 · Convenience-store picnic: ramyeon &amp; kimbap (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Picnic at the river<br><b>Korean · convenience store</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩5–8k per person, food only<br>The classic Han River ritual: cook instant ramyeon at the CU's hot-water machine, grab kimbap and a can, and sit on the steps as the lights come on.<br><i>≈₩8k for two and genuinely one of the best-loved Seoul experiences.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h<br>• Add an egg and cheese slice — locals do.<br>• Rent a mat from the kiosk (≈₩3k) if the grass is damp.<br>Naver: 뚝섬한강공원 편의점]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0675,37.5308,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sat 31 Oct 19:30 · Hangang Drone Light Show — Marvel finale, from Ttukseom (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Drone show — where to watch<br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>The season finale of Seoul's Hangang drone light show: 2,000 drones over the river in a Marvel-themed programme, 19:30–21:25 with the main show around 20:30.<br><b>Hours:</b> 19:30–21:25 · main show ≈20:30<br>• Sat 31 Oct is the finale — expect crowds; claim your spot by 19:00.<br>• Walk home ≈25 min; taxis are scarce right after the show.<br>Naver: 뚝섬한강공원]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.069,37.53,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sat 31 Oct 19:30 · Same show from the quieter Jamsil bank (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Drone show — where to watch<br><b>Show · across the river</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>The drones rise over the river between the two parks, so the Jamsil Hangang Park side sees the same 2,000-drone finale — with far fewer people and the Seongsu skyline as backdrop.<br><i>Same show, a fraction of the crowd, and the whole thing reflects in the water.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> 19:30–21:25 · main show ≈20:30<br>• Taxi from Seoul Forest ≈12 min over Yeongdong Bridge; ask for 잠실한강공원.<br>• Taxi back to Konkuk ≈15 min — book on Kakao T before the finale ends.<br>Naver: 잠실한강공원]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0824,37.5178,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sun 01 Nov 10:00 · Sleep in (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Slow Sunday morning<br><b>Rest</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>No alarm. Coffee from the convenience store, a lie-in, and out by noon.<br><i>It's Sunday.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> —<br>Naver: 하루포레스트호텔 성수&amp;건대점]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0697,37.5405,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sun 01 Nov 10:00 · Korean sauna at Siloam Sauna, Junggok-dong (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Slow Sunday morning<br><b>Jjimjilbang · 1.5–2 h</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩10–15k per person<br>A basic neighbourhood Korean bathhouse: hot and cold pools, a dry sauna and a scrub station, with separate men's and women's baths.<br><i>The real, unglamorous Korean Sunday-morning ritual.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; entry fee at the desk includes towels and clothes.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈05:00–22:00<br>• Baths are naked and gender-separated; meet again in the mixed jjimjil room in the sauna clothes.<br>• Taxi ≈10 min from the hotel.<br>Naver: 중곡동 실로암사우나]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0829,37.5628,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sun 01 Nov 12:30 · Common Ground (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Brunch near Konkuk<br><b>Food court · burgers, pasta, Korean</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩12–18k per person, food only<br>Two hundred blue shipping containers stacked into a mall of small brands, with a food terrace of burgers, pasta and Korean bowls on the top deck.<br><i>Sunday market stalls in the courtyard.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 11:00–22:00<br>• Five minutes' walk from the hotel.<br>• Good coffee at the ground-floor cafés.<br>Naver: 커먼그라운드]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0662,37.541,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sun 01 Nov 12:30 · Cafe Onion Seongsu (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Brunch near Konkuk<br><b>Bakery-café · pastries &amp; brunch</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩10–14k per person, food only<br>The factory-turned-bakery of Seongsu; if you missed it yesterday, Sunday brunch here is the move.<br><i>Pandoro pastry and the courtyard.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; queue at the counter on Sundays.<br><b>Hours:</b> 10:00–22:00 (Sun)<br>• Two pastries + two coffees ≈₩25k total.<br>Naver: 카페 어니언 성수]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0575,37.5445,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sun 01 Nov 14:30 · Seokchon Lake Park (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Lake or lone tree<br><b>Park · 2.5 km loop</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>Two lakes ringed by a promenade in Songpa, with Lotte World's castle on one side and the 555 m tower above everything.<br><i>Cherry trees turning red in early November.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h<br>• Walk the east lake, then the tower is a 5-min stroll.<br>Naver: 석촌호수]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.1051525,37.5113096,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sun 01 Nov 14:30 · Olympic Park lone tree (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Lake or lone tree<br><b>Park · walk</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>A single tree on a vast lawn inside the 1988 Olympic Park — Seoul's favourite couple-photo spot, ringed by the ancient earthen Mongchon fortress.<br><i>Wild-flower hill and the fortress walk if you have energy.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 05:00–22:00<br>• From the tree, the tower is a 15-min taxi or Line 8 back to Jamsil.<br>Naver: 올림픽공원 나홀로나무]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.1265,37.5188,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sun 01 Nov 16:45 · Seoul Sky observatory (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Seoul Sky<br><b>Observatory · 500 m up</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩31k per person (online ≈₩29k)<br>The observatory of Lotte World Tower — the world's fifth-tallest building — occupies floors 117–123, up to 500 m above the lake.<br><i>Sky Deck glass floor on the 118th; café on the 123rd.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> Book the 16:45 slot online to skip the ticket queue.<br><b>Hours:</b> 10:30–22:00 (Fri–Sat to 23:00)<br>• Go up 45 min before the 17:35 sunset and stay for the lights.<br>Naver: 서울스카이]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.102305,37.5125295,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sun 01 Nov 16:45 · Seoul Sky + Sky Bridge Tour (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Seoul Sky<br><b>Observatory + outdoor bridge walk</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩100k per person (includes observatory)<br>A harnessed walk across the open-air bridge between the tower's two spires at 541 m — the highest building bridge in the world — then the observatory.<br><i>Only a handful of slots a day; you get the photos and a certificate.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> Book well ahead on the Seoul Sky site; weather-dependent, refunds if cancelled.<br><b>Hours:</b> Tours ≈10:00–19:00<br>• Splurge option — it's ≈€60 each; the standard observatory is ₩31k.<br>• No loose items on the bridge; phones go in a provided case.<br>Naver: 서울스카이 스카이브릿지]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.102305,37.5125295,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sun 01 Nov 19:00 · Geumgeum, Songridan-gil (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Dinner<br><b>Korean bistro · rice bowls &amp; small plates</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩15–22k per person, food only<br>A cosy neighbourhood spot in the low-rise lanes east of Seokchon Lake — Songridan-gil is Seoul's answer to Tokyo's back alleys, full of small kitchens and wine bars.<br><i>Ten-minute walk from the tower; the whole street is worth a wander after.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> Walk in; check the Naver page for today's menu and hours.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈17:00–22:00<br>• Line 2 back to Konkuk Univ., 4 stops.<br>Naver: 금금 송리단길]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.1062,37.5085,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sun 01 Nov 19:00 · Konkuk lamb-skewer street (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Dinner<br><b>Chinese-Korean · lamb skewers grilled at the table</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩18–25k per person, food only<br>Behind Konkuk Univ. station, a whole street of yangkkochi houses: cumin-dusted lamb skewers turning on automatic grills, with Tsingtao and stir-fried lotus root.<br><i>Loud, cheap, student energy — and it's next to your hotel.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈17:00–02:00<br>• 10 skewers (≈₩15k) + one fried dish between two is plenty.<br>• Konkuk Univ. station exit 5, 3 min.<br>Naver: 건대 양꼬치 거리]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0683,37.5396,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Busan · Hotel Aqua Palace (2–5 Nov)</name>
<Placemark><name>Hotel: Hotel Aqua Palace</name><styleUrl>#hotel</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>2–5 Nov</b><br>A Gwangalli beachfront hotel with a Korean bathhouse; the 21st-floor deck faces the Gwangan Bridge.<br>Check-in 15:00 · out 11:00]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1188051,35.1540793,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mon 02 Nov 09:00 · Taxi to Seoul Station with the bags (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> To Seoul Station<br><b>Price:</b> Taxi ≈₩20k<br>The terminus for the KTX to Busan, a landmark since 1925 — the old red-brick station stands beside the modern glass one.<br><b>Hours:</b> Allow 30 min + a 20 min buffer<br>• Lockers, cafés and a snack floor inside for the train.<br>• KTX platforms are on the ground level — check the board for your train number.<br>Naver: 서울역]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.972317,37.555946,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mon 02 Nov 09:00 · Metro Line 2 → Line 4 with the bags (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> To Seoul Station<br><b>Metro · ≈35 min · ₩1,550</b><br><b>Price:</b> ₩1,550 per person<br>Konkuk Univ. → Line 2 to Dongdaemun History &amp; Culture Park → Line 4 to Seoul Station: two trains, one easy change, elevators at both ends.<br><i>Cheap and traffic-proof — worth it if the bags roll well.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> First trains 05:30<br>• Leave by 08:45 for the 10:00 train — buffer for the ticket gates.<br>• Seoul Station exit 1 / 2 lead straight to the KTX hall.<br>Naver: 서울역]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9707,37.5547,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mon 02 Nov 10:00 · KTX Seoul → Busan (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Train to Busan<br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩60k per person<br>Korea's high-speed train (up to 305 km/h) covers the 417 km to Busan in under three hours, past Daejeon, Daegu and the Gyeongju hills.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈2 h 40 min<br>• Book at letskorail.com or the Korail Talk app as soon as sales open.<br>• Snack cart and vending in car 4; Wi-Fi on board.<br>Naver: 부산역]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.042243,35.115225,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mon 02 Nov 10:00 · SRT from Suseo → Busan (back-up) (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Train to Busan<br><b>High-speed rail · ≈2 h 20</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩52,000 per person<br>The second high-speed line leaves from Suseo in the south-east: slightly cheaper and faster, handy if the KTX sells out — but Suseo is 35 min by metro from Konkuk.<br><i>A quieter station and ₩8k cheaper per seat.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> Roughly hourly<br>• Book at srtrain.co.kr (foreign cards work).<br>• Line 3 or the Bundang line to Suseo; allow an hour door to door.<br>Naver: 수서역]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.1016,37.4871,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mon 02 Nov 12:45 · Choryang Milmyeon (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Lunch at Busan Station<br><b>Busan · wheat cold noodles</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩9–13k per person, food only<br>Busan's own noodle: chewy wheat noodles in an icy beef broth with a dab of spicy sauce, born in the refugee kitchens of the 1950s. This is the branch Busan people send you to.<br><i>Milmyeon exists only in Busan — start the visit with it.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; quick turnover.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈10:00–20:00<br>• 'Mul' (broth) or 'bibim' (dry, spicy) — get one of each and swap.<br>• Add a plate of mandu (₩6k).<br>Naver: 초량밀면]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.0407,35.1163,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mon 02 Nov 12:45 · Bonjeon Dwaeji Gukbap (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Lunch at Busan Station<br><b>Busan · pork &amp; rice soup</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩10–13k per person, food only<br>Right across from the station: the Michelin Bib Gourmand dwaeji-gukbap that Busanites queue for — a milky, clean pork broth over rice, seasoned with salted shrimp and chives.<br><i>Busan's comfort food at its most famous address.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; 10–20 min queue at lunch.<br><b>Hours:</b> 09:00–21:00<br>• One bowl each (≈₩10k); add a side of suyuk (boiled pork) to share if hungry.<br>• Season with saeu-jeot (salted shrimp) and a spoon of the chives.<br>Naver: 본전돼지국밥]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.041,35.1157,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mon 02 Nov 14:00 · Check in at Hotel Aqua Palace, rest</name><styleUrl>#Busan</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[A Gwangalli beachfront hotel with a Korean bathhouse; the 21st-floor deck faces the Gwangan Bridge.<br><b>Hours:</b> Check-in 15:00 · out 11:00<br>• Ask for a bridge-view room.<br>• The hotel bathhouse is naked and gender-separated; swimsuits for the pool.<br>Naver: 아쿠아팰리스호텔]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1188051,35.1540793,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mon 02 Nov 16:15 · Millak Waterside Park (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Sunset over the bridge<br><b>Waterfront</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>Wide stone steps down to the water at the eastern end of Gwangalli — the best free seat for the 7.4 km Gwangan Bridge at dusk.<br><i>Beer and snacks from the convenience store behind you.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h · sunset 17:26<br>• Sit on the steps facing the bridge; it lights up at dusk.<br>• The drone show is Saturdays only — you get the bridge lights anyway.<br>Naver: 민락수변공원]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1329907,35.1545716,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mon 02 Nov 16:15 · Igidae coastal walk (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Sunset over the bridge<br><b>Coastal path · 2 km</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>A boardwalk and cliff path on the headland south of Gwangalli, looking straight back at the bridge and the Haeundae skyline across the bay.<br><i>The best angle on the bridge at sunset, with the sea below you.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h · sunset 17:26<br>• Start at the Dongsaengmal viewpoint (taxi drop-off) and walk 20 min north for the views.<br>• Some steps — wear trainers.<br>Naver: 이기대 해안산책로]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1215,35.131,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mon 02 Nov 18:00 · Millak Fisherman's Market — raw fish upstairs (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Dinner on Gwangalli<br><b>Seafood · raw fish (hoe) with a view</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩30–35k per person, food only · ₩5k pp table charge<br>Pick a live fish from a vendor downstairs, carry it upstairs, and eat it as sashimi facing the lit-up Gwangan Bridge — the Busan dinner.<br><i>Bridge view, plastic chairs, the freshest fish in the city.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None. Set a budget with the vendor first (≈₩50–60k of fish for two) and confirm the ₩5k-per-person seating charge upstairs.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈10:00–24:00<br>• Budget tip: say '5만원어치' (₩50k worth) and stick to it — flounder and sea bream are the value picks.<br>• Finish with maeuntang (spicy fish stew) made from the bones, ≈₩10k.<br>Naver: 민락어민활어직판장]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1315,35.1545,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mon 02 Nov 18:00 · Gwangan Jeil Dwaeji Gukbap (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Dinner on Gwangalli<br><b>Busan · pork &amp; rice soup</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩10–13k per person, food only<br>The neighbourhood gukbap house two blocks behind the beach — a clean, rich pork broth and a menu you can read in ten seconds.<br><i>Open late, cheap, and five minutes from the hotel.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈24 h<br>• Add suyuk-baekban (boiled pork with rice) if one of you wants something less soupy.<br>Naver: 광안제일돼지국밥]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1172,35.1533,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mon 02 Nov 20:00 · Night yacht on Suyeongman Bay (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Night on the bay<br><b>Night sailing · 50 min</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩30k per person<br>A 50-minute sail under the lit Gwangan Bridge and along the Marine City towers from the 1988 Olympic yachting centre — the bay at night from the water.<br><i>Under the lit bridge from the water; Marine City towers overhead.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> Book on Trazy or Klook; weekday sailings hourly 18:00–21:00.<br><b>Hours:</b> Weekday sailings hourly 18:00–21:00<br>• Book on Trazy or Klook; pick the 20:00 sailing.<br>• Cold on deck — bring the layer.<br>• Taxi back to the hotel ≈8 min, ≈₩6k.<br>Naver: 수영만요트경기장]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1405,35.156,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mon 02 Nov 20:00 · Gwangalli beach night walk &amp; bridge lights (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Night on the bay<br><b>Night walk</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free · beer ≈₩4k<br>The 1.4 km beach promenade after dark with the whole Gwangan Bridge lit in changing colours, then a can of beer on the sand.<br><i>Zero cost, five minutes from the hotel; the bridge light show runs nightly.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h · bridge lit until ≈24:00<br>• Walk east to Millak and back.<br>• 'Beer street' is one block behind the beach for a nightcap.<br>Naver: 광안리해수욕장]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1187,35.1531,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mon 02 Nov 20:00 · Haeundae Blueline beach train at night (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Night on the bay<br><b>Night ride · 30 min</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩7k per person one way<br>The slow beach train along the lit coast from Mipo to Cheongsapo and back — the same line as tomorrow's capsule, from the other carriage.<br><i>Harbour lights and the dark sea; far fewer people than by day.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> Walk-up; last trains ≈21:00 in November — check the day's timetable.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈09:30–21:00<br>• Taxi ≈15 min from Gwangalli.<br>• If you ride tonight, tomorrow's capsule still feels different — it's the view down.<br>Naver: 해운대 블루라인파크]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.173,35.1587,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tue 03 Nov 10:00 · Sky Capsule, Mipo → Cheongsapo (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Along the Blueline coast<br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩35k per 2-person capsule one way<br>Private four-seat pods gliding 7–10 m above the shoreline on the old railway line from Mipo to Cheongsapo — Busan's most photographed ride.<br><b>Hours:</b> 09:30–17:30 (Nov)<br>• Bookings open Tue 6 Oct 09:00 KST = 02:00 Munich — the 10:00 slot sells out fast.<br>• Sit on the sea side; the colourful pods over the water are the photo.<br>Naver: 블루라인파크 미포정거장]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1727672,35.158284,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tue 03 Nov 10:00 · Blueline Beach Train instead (cheaper, no booking) (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Along the Blueline coast<br><b>Coastal train · 30 min</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩7,000 one way per person<br>The slow red beach train runs the same clifftop track as the capsules, with big windows facing the sea; hop on at Mipo and off at Cheongsapo.<br><i>Walk-up tickets, same views, a third of the price.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> 09:30–18:00 (Nov)<br>• Sit on the sea side (left going north).<br>• If capsules are sold out, this is the plan — no booking needed.<br>Naver: 해운대 해변열차]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1645,35.1596,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tue 03 Nov 11:00 · Cheongsapo Daritdol Skywalk (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Cheongsapo<br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>A 72 m glass-bottomed walkway jutting out over the rocks beside a fishing village with twin red-and-white lighthouses.<br><b>Hours:</b> 09:00–17:00 (Nov)<br>• Shoe covers are provided for the glass section.<br>• The lighthouses are 5 min back toward the harbour.<br>Naver: 청사포 다릿돌전망대]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1967173,35.1640365,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tue 03 Nov 11:00 · Cheongsapo harbour &amp; twin lighthouses (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Cheongsapo<br><b>Harbour stroll</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>A tiny fishing harbour with a red and a white lighthouse at the end of the breakwaters, drying seaweed on the quay and cafés tucked above the rocks.<br><i>Walk out to the red lighthouse — the view back to the village is pure Busan.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> Always open<br>• Grilled clams restaurants here are the lunch option next.<br>• Watch the slippery breakwater steps after rain.<br>Naver: 청사포 쌍둥이등대]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1915,35.1588,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tue 03 Nov 12:00 · Suminine — grilled shellfish, smallest set (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Lunch in Cheongsapo<br><b>Seafood · grilled clams (jogae-gui)</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩30–35k per person, food only<br>The harbour's grilled-shellfish house: a tray of clams, scallops and abalone over charcoal at your table, with cheese-baked scallops and a kimchi-fried-rice finish.<br><i>Sea view from the second floor; the whole harbour smells of it.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; sets are for two.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈11:00–21:00<br>• Budget tip: order the smallest set (≈₩60–70k for two) and skip the abalone upgrade.<br>• Ask for the kimchi bokkeumbap at the end — cooked in the shell juices.<br>Naver: 청사포 수민이네]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1952,35.1633,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tue 03 Nov 12:00 · Geumsu Bokguk — regular soup (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Lunch in Cheongsapo<br><b>Busan · pufferfish soup</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩15–20k per person, food only<br>Busan's most famous bokguk (pufferfish soup) since 1970: a clear, restorative broth with bean sprouts and water parsley — the city's classic hangover cure.<br><i>Open 24 hours; the safest, cleanest way to try fugu.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h<br>• Budget tip: the regular 'eun-bok' soup (≈₩16k) — the premium fish versions run to ₩40k+.<br>• Taxi ≈12 min from Cheongsapo, on the way to the temple.<br>Naver: 금수복국 해운대본점]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1595,35.1625,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tue 03 Nov 13:30 · Haedong Yonggungsa Temple (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Afternoon on the north coast<br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>A Buddhist temple from 1376 built on the rocks above the waves in Gijang — one of very few Korean temples on the coast rather than in the mountains.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈05:00–sunset<br>• 108 steps down to the temple; rub the golden pigs for luck.<br>• Weekends are packed — a Tuesday is ideal.<br>Naver: 해동용궁사]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.2231109,35.1884543,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tue 03 Nov 13:30 · Songjeong Beach &amp; Jukdo Park (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Afternoon on the north coast<br><b>Beach walk</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>Busan's laid-back surf beach, two stops up the coast: a wide crescent of sand, a wooden boardwalk, surf cafés and the little pine-covered Jukdo islet at the end.<br><i>Empty sand in November and the best flat-white on the coast.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> Always open<br>• Taxi from Cheongsapo ≈8 min.<br>• Jukdo Park pavilion at the north end is the sunset spot.<br>Naver: 송정해수욕장]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1998,35.1786,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tue 03 Nov 16:15 · BUSAN X the SKY (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Sunset over Haeundae<br><b>Observatory · 100th floor</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩27k per person<br>The observatory on floors 98–100 of the LCT tower at the east end of Haeundae beach — the beach, Gwangan Bridge and the coast you rode this morning, from 384 m.<br><i>Starbucks on the 99th floor is the highest in the world.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> Walk-up or online; sunset slots are busiest.<br><b>Hours:</b> 10:00–21:00 · last entry 20:30<br>• Be up by 16:45 for the 17:25 sunset.<br>• Taxi ≈20 min from the temple.<br>Naver: 부산엑스더스카이]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1668,35.1601,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tue 03 Nov 16:15 · Dongbaekseom island walk (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Sunset over Haeundae<br><b>Coastal loop · 1.5 km</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>The pine-covered headland at the west end of Haeundae: a flat boardwalk loop around the island with the APEC House, a mermaid statue and the bridge across the bay.<br><i>Sunset from the western rocks, then the beach lights on the walk back.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h · sunset 17:25<br>• Start from the Westin/Chosun side and walk anticlockwise.<br>• Taxi ≈25 min from the temple.<br>Naver: 동백섬]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.153,35.1535,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tue 03 Nov 18:30 · Sangguginae, Haeundae Market (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Dinner in Haeundae<br><b>Busan street food · tteokbokki, fish cake, gimbap</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩8–12k per person, food only<br>The most famous stall in Haeundae Market: tteokbokki, eomuk skewers in broth, fried gimbap and sundae eaten standing at the counter.<br><i>Loud, hot, ₩10k for two — Busan snack culture in one stop.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; short queue.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈09:00–24:00<br>• Add the grilled eel restaurants next door if you're still hungry (≈₩20k).<br>• Taxi home ≈15 min.<br>Naver: 상국이네]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1605,35.1612,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tue 03 Nov 18:30 · Geumsu Bokguk (24 h) (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Dinner in Haeundae<br><b>Busan · pufferfish soup</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩15–20k per person, food only<br>If you had clams at lunch, dinner is the bokguk you skipped — a clear pufferfish broth that's as good at night as it is in the morning.<br><i>Open 24 h, 5 min from the beach.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h<br>• Regular soup ≈₩16k; skip the premium fish upgrades.<br>Naver: 금수복국 해운대본점]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1595,35.1625,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tue 03 Nov 20:00 · The Bay 101 — Marine City night view (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Evening in Haeundae<br><b>Night view · café</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free · drinks ≈₩8k<br>A yacht-club deck opposite the Marine City towers, which mirror in the still water of the marina after dark — Busan's postcard night shot.<br><i>Fish and chips and a beer on the deck; the reflections are the photo.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈11:00–24:00<br>• Taxi ≈7 min from Haeundae Market.<br>• Then a taxi home ≈15 min.<br>Naver: 더베이101]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.152,35.156,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tue 03 Nov 20:00 · Haeundae beach lights &amp; Gunam-ro (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Evening in Haeundae<br><b>Night walk</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>The beach after dark with the LCT tower lit above it, then the busy Gunam-ro pedestrian street of buskers and bars.<br><i>Street performers most evenings around the Gunam-ro fountain.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h<br>• Five minutes from the market.<br>• Taxi home ≈15 min.<br>Naver: 해운대해수욕장]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1604,35.1587,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tue 03 Nov 20:00 · Busan Cinema Center LED roof (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Evening in Haeundae<br><b>Architecture</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>The world's longest cantilever roof, a 163 m wing of LEDs that plays light sequences over the plaza where the Busan film festival opens.<br><i>Quiet, odd and beautiful; ten minutes from Spa Land.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> Roof lights until ≈22:00<br>• Taxi ≈12 min from Haeundae.<br>• Taxi home to Gwangalli ≈10 min.<br>Naver: 영화의전당]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1274,35.1716,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 04 Nov 09:30 · Gamcheon Culture Village (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Old Busan morning<br><b>Price:</b> Free · stamp map ≈₩2,000<br>A 1950s refugee settlement of pastel houses stacked up the hillside, turned into an open-air art village in 2009.<br><b>Hours:</b> 09:00–18:00<br>• Buy the stamp map at the info centre and collect stamps for a free postcard.<br>• The Little Prince viewpoint has a queue — go there first.<br>• Steep stairs: good shoes.<br>Naver: 감천문화마을]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.0105921,35.0973935,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 04 Nov 09:30 · Bosu-dong Book Alley &amp; Bupyeong Kkangtong Market (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Old Busan morning<br><b>Old town walk</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>A lane of 50 second-hand bookshops stacked to the ceilings, then the covered Kkangtong ("tin can") market next door — Busan as it was before the murals.<br><i>Real old Busan with zero tour buses, and it's on the way to Jagalchi.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> Shops ≈10:00–19:00<br>• Korean picture books make lovely cheap gifts.<br>• Kkangtong Market has a night-market street from 18:00 — different vibe.<br>Naver: 보수동 책방골목]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.0227,35.1041,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 04 Nov 12:00 · Jagalchi Market 2F grilled fish + BIFF hotteok (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Lunch at Nampo<br><b>Seafood · grilled fish set, then a seed pancake</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩12–20k per person, food only<br>Korea's largest fish market: on the second floor, restaurants grill mackerel, hairtail and eel over charcoal and serve them as a set with rice and soup. Then walk 5 min to BIFF Square for ssiat-hotteok.<br><i>Grilled-fish set (₩12–15k) beats buying live fish for value; the seed-stuffed hotteok (₩2k) is Busan's iconic snack.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None. Closed 1st &amp; 3rd Tuesday.<br><b>Hours:</b> 05:00–22:00<br>• Budget tip: order the 'saengseon-gui jeongsik' (grilled fish set) rather than by-weight fish.<br>• Nampo station exit 2 for BIFF Square.<br>Naver: 자갈치시장]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.0307965,35.0966339,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 04 Nov 12:00 · Halmae Gaya Milmyeon (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Lunch at Nampo<br><b>Busan · wheat cold noodles</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩8–12k per person, food only<br>A Nampo-dong milmyeon house from the 1970s, two minutes from BIFF Square — plain bowls, icy broth, no fuss.<br><i>The Nampo locals' lunch; ₩8k bowls.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈10:00–21:00<br>• Order 'mul-milmyeon' and a plate of mandu.<br>• Then hotteok at BIFF Square anyway.<br>Naver: 할매가야밀면]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.0292,35.0985,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 04 Nov 14:30 · Huinnyeoul Culture Village (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Afternoon by the sea<br><b>Cliff village · walk &amp; cafés</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free · coffee ≈₩6k<br>White-washed houses on a cliff above the sea on Yeongdo island, with a coastal path, a tunnel through the rock and cafés hanging over the water.<br><i>Stay for the 17:24 sunset over the anchored ships.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> Cafés ≈11:00–20:00<br>• Taxi ≈15 min across the Yeongdo bridge.<br>• Featured in the film 'The Attorney' — hence the murals.<br>Naver: 흰여울문화마을]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.0453198,35.0782798,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 04 Nov 14:30 · Songdo Marine Cable Car (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Afternoon by the sea<br><b>Cable car · 1.6 km over the sea</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩17k return (standard) · ₩22k crystal floor<br>Cabins crossing 1.6 km of open water from Songdo beach to Amnam Park's cliffs, with a coastal skywalk and a cliff trail on the far side.<br><i>Crystal-floor cabins look straight down at the sea.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> Walk-up.<br><b>Hours:</b> 09:00–21:00<br>• Taxi ≈10 min from Jagalchi.<br>• Walk Amnam Park's cliff path for 30 min before riding back.<br>Naver: 송도해상케이블카]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.0174,35.0762,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 04 Nov 14:30 · Taejongdae (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Afternoon by the sea<br><b>Cliffs &amp; lighthouse park</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free · Danubi train ≈₩4k<br>The wooded southern tip of Yeongdo: 100 m sea cliffs, a lighthouse and an observatory looking out to where Tsushima appears on clear days.<br><i>The Danubi road train loops the park so you don't have to climb.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 04:00–24:00 · train 09:20–17:30<br>• Taxi ≈25 min from Jagalchi.<br>• Weekday afternoons are quiet.<br>Naver: 태종대]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.087,35.053,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 04 Nov 18:30 · Spa Land Centum City (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Evening soak<br><b>Spa · 13 saunas &amp; hot springs</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩20–25k per person (weekday)<br>Korea's most luxurious jjimjilbang, inside the world's largest department store: 22 hot-spring baths from two natural sources, 13 themed saunas, an open-air foot spa and a relaxation lounge.<br><i>The Roman bath and the Finnish sauna; couples meet in the mixed sauna zone in the spa uniform.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; 4-hour ticket. Last entry 21:00.<br><b>Hours:</b> 06:00–22:00 · last entry 21:00<br>• Taxi ≈25 min from Yeongdo, ≈10 min from the hotel afterwards.<br>• Baths are naked and gender-separated; the themed saunas are mixed.<br>Naver: 스파랜드 센텀시티]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1302,35.169,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 04 Nov 18:30 · Hotel spa &amp; beach walk (option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Evening soak<br><b>Rest · hotel bathhouse</b><br><b>Price:</b> Spa ≈₩15k per person<br>Back to Gwangalli for the hotel's own bathhouse, then a slow walk along the beach under the bridge lights.<br><i>No travel, bridge view, bed nearby.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> Spa ≈06:00–22:00<br>• Gwangalli has a 'beer street' one block behind the beach for a nightcap.<br>Naver: 아쿠아팰리스호텔]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1188051,35.1540793,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 04 Nov 21:00 · Gwangan Jeil Dwaeji Gukbap (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Late dinner: gukbap<br><b>Busan · pork &amp; rice soup</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩10–13k per person, food only<br>The neighbourhood gukbap house behind Gwangalli beach — open late and five minutes from the hotel.<br><i>A hot bowl at 21:00 after the spa.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈24 h<br>• Add suyuk-baekban if you want something less soupy.<br>Naver: 광안제일돼지국밥]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.1172,35.1533,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wed 04 Nov 21:00 · 1953 Hyeongje Dwaeji Gukbap, Seomyeon (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Busan-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Late dinner: gukbap<br><b>Busan · pork &amp; rice soup</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩11–14k per person, food only<br>On Seomyeon's 'gukbap alley': a 70-year-old kitchen serving a milkier, richer style of the soup, open around the clock.<br><i>The oldest name on the alley; Seomyeon's neon streets outside.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h<br>• Line 2 from Centum City to Seomyeon ≈20 min; taxi home from Seomyeon ≈20 min.<br>Naver: 형제돼지국밥 서면]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.0607,35.1566,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Seoul · glue hotel (5–7 Nov)</name>
<Placemark><name>Hotel: glue hotel</name><styleUrl>#hotel</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>5–7 Nov</b><br>A design hotel at 228 Yulgok-ro, Jongno, at the foot of Naksan — theatre district by day, fortress wall by night. Live music in the lounge ≈20:00–22:00.<br>Check-in 15:00 · out 11:00]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0039779,37.5758065,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 05 Nov 11:00 · Samjin Eomuk at Busan Station (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Snacks for the train<br><b>Busan · fish cakes</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩6–10k per person, food only<br>Busan's oldest fish-cake maker (1953) runs a bakery-style shop inside the station: trays of warm fish cakes stuffed with cheese, shrimp or peppers — the train picnic.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈07:00–21:00<br>• Buy 5–6 pieces and a coffee; eat them on the train.<br>• Card accepted.<br>Naver: 삼진어묵 부산역점]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.0416,35.1152,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 05 Nov 11:00 · Station food court: kimbap, coffee &amp; pastries (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Snacks for the train<br><b>Food court · quick bites</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩6–9k per person<br>The second floor of Busan Station has a food court and bakeries — kimbap rolls, egg sandwiches, coffee — right next to the KTX gates.<br><i>Fast, cheap and you can eat it on the train.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> ≈07:00–21:00<br>• Buy two coffees — KTX has no café car.<br>• Convenience store at platform level if you're late.<br>Naver: 부산역 2층 식당가]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.0416,35.1152,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 05 Nov 11:30 · KTX Busan → Seoul (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Train back to Seoul<br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩60k per person<br>The same route in reverse — back in Seoul by early afternoon.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈2 h 40 min<br>• Platforms are upstairs at Busan Station; check the board.<br>Naver: 서울역]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.972317,37.555946,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 05 Nov 11:30 · SRT Busan → Suseo (back-up) (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Train back to Seoul<br><b>High-speed rail · ≈2 h 20</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩52,000 per person<br>If the KTX is full, the SRT reaches Suseo in south-east Seoul in 2 h 20; from Suseo the glue hotel is ≈40 min by metro (Line 3 → Jongno 3-ga) or a 35-min taxi.<br><i>Slightly cheaper and often has seats when KTX is sold out.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> Roughly hourly<br>• Book at srtrain.co.kr.<br>• From Suseo take Line 3 to Anguk and a 10-min taxi to the hotel.<br>Naver: 부산역]]></description><Point><coordinates>129.0416,35.1152,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 05 Nov 14:30 · Check in at glue hotel</name><styleUrl>#Seoul</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[A design hotel at 228 Yulgok-ro, Jongno, at the foot of Naksan — theatre district by day, fortress wall by night. Live music in the lounge ≈20:00–22:00.<br><b>Hours:</b> Check-in 15:00 · out 11:00<br>• Rooftop faces Naksan; the lounge has live sets ≈20:00–22:00.<br>Naver: 글루호텔]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0039779,37.5758065,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 05 Nov 16:40 · Naksan fortress wall at sunset (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Naksan at sunset<br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>A section of the 600-year-old Seoul City Wall climbs Naksan hill behind the hotel — murals on the way up, the whole skyline from the top.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h · wall lit until ≈23:00<br>• Walk up through Ihwa Mural Village.<br>• Naksan Park terrace has the best downtown skyline view.<br>Naver: 낙산공원 성곽길]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0089876,37.5760794,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 05 Nov 16:40 · Ihwa Mural Village lanes (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Naksan at sunset<br><b>Hillside village</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>The steep lanes below the fortress wall: stair murals, tiny cafés, rooftop views over Dongdaemun — the same sunset from a lower, cosier angle.<br><i>Flowers-on-stairs photo spot and a rooftop café for the sunset.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> Always open (quiet after 20:00, residents live here)<br>• Keep voices down — it's a residential hill.<br>• Café 개뿔 rooftop faces the sunset.<br>Naver: 이화벽화마을]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0056,37.5802,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 05 Nov 18:30 · Sunhui-ne Bindaetteok (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Dinner at Gwangjang Market<br><b>Market food · mung-bean pancakes</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩8–12k per person, food only<br>The market's most famous bindaetteok stall: mung beans ground in front of you and fried in a pool of oil, eaten on a plastic stool with makgeolli.<br><i>Netflix-famous, and still ₩5k a pancake.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None — find a stool.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈09:00–23:00<br>• Two pancakes and a bowl of makgeolli (≈₩5k) between you.<br>• Mostly cash.<br>Naver: 순희네빈대떡 광장시장]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9996,37.57,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 05 Nov 18:30 · Buchon Yukhoe (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Dinner at Gwangjang Market<br><b>Korean · raw beef tartare</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩15–22k per person, food only<br>On Gwangjang's yukhoe alley: hand-cut raw beef with pear, sesame oil and a raw egg yolk — Michelin Bib Gourmand and the best-known of the alley's shops.<br><i>Order the yukhoe-bibimbap for something more filling.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; 10–20 min queue at dinner.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈10:00–22:00<br>• Budget tip: one plate of yukhoe (≈₩20k) shared plus a bibimbap keeps it under ₩20k each.<br>• Then a bindaetteok for dessert anyway.<br>Naver: 부촌육회 광장시장]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0001,37.5699,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 05 Nov 20:00 · Live music in the hotel lounge (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Evening<br><b>Music · free</b><br><b>Price:</b> Drinks ≈₩8–15k<br>The hotel lounge hosts small live sets most evenings ≈20:00–22:00 — a soft landing after the market.<br><i>Zero commute.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> Ask the front desk for tonight's set.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈20:00–22:00<br>Naver: 글루호텔]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0039779,37.5758065,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 05 Nov 20:00 · DDP at night (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Evening<br><b>Architecture</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>Zaha Hadid's silver spaceship of a building, lit at night, with the LED rose garden on the lawn and Dongdaemun's 24-hour fashion malls around it.<br><i>Ten minutes' walk from Gwangjang Market; the roses glow after dark.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> Exterior 24 h<br>• Taxi back to the hotel ≈7 min.<br>Naver: 동대문디자인플라자]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0092,37.5665,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thu 05 Nov 20:00 · A small Daehangno play (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Evening<br><b>Theatre · 90 min</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩20–40k per person<br>Daehangno has over a hundred small theatres around Hyehwa station; comedies and musicals with easy plots work even without Korean.<br><i>Tickets at the booth on the Marronnier Park square; same-day discounts are common.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> Same-day at the ticket booth, or Interpark for English listings.<br><b>Hours:</b> Evening shows ≈19:30–20:00<br>• Five minutes' walk from the hotel.<br>• Pick a non-verbal show (Nanta-style) if you want zero language barrier.<br>Naver: 대학로 소극장]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0025,37.5822,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 06 Nov 10:00 · Changdeokgung &amp; Secret Garden tour (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Changdeokgung morning<br><b>Price:</b> Palace ₩3,000 + garden ₩5,000<br>A UNESCO-listed palace laid into the hillside, with the 32-hectare Huwon (Secret Garden) of pavilions and lotus ponds behind it — the loveliest place in Seoul in autumn.<br><b>Hours:</b> 09:00–17:30 (Nov) · garden by guided tour only · <b>Closed:</b> Mondays<br>• Garden tickets are released 6 days ahead at royal.khs.go.kr — 50 online, 50 on-site per slot.<br>• Peak colour in the garden: late Oct – early Nov.<br>Naver: 창덕궁 후원]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9931659,37.5821721,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 06 Nov 10:00 · Changdeokgung palace only + Bukchon viewpoint (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Changdeokgung morning<br><b>Palace · no tour booking</b><br><b>Price:</b> ₩3,000 per person<br>Skip the timed garden tour and wander the main palace at your own pace — the most beautiful of the five — then climb to the Bukchon 6th view for the hanok-roofs-and-palace photo.<br><i>No slot to chase; the Injeongjeon hall and Nakseonjae are stunning on their own.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> 09:00–17:30 · closed Mon<br>• Still free with the 4-palace pass if you bought one.<br>• Bukchon-ro 11-gil for the classic view, 10 min walk.<br>Naver: 창덕궁]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.991,37.5794,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 06 Nov 12:00 · Changgyeonggung glasshouse (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Midday around the palaces<br><b>Price:</b> ₩1,000<br>The quieter palace next door: a 1909 Victorian glasshouse (Korea's first), the Chundangji pond you saw lit up on Thursday, and almost no crowds.<br><b>Hours:</b> 09:00–21:00 · <b>Closed:</b> Mondays<br>• The glasshouse is at the far north end past the pond.<br>• Exit through Honghwamun onto Changgyeonggung-ro.<br>Naver: 창경궁 대온실]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.996,37.579,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 06 Nov 12:00 · Ikseon-dong hanok alleys (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Midday around the palaces<br><b>Cafés · shops · hanok lanes</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free (coffee ≈₩7k)<br>A maze of 1920s hanok turned into tiny cafés, tea rooms and shops a few minutes south of the palace — Seoul's prettiest alleys for an unhurried hour.<br><i>Low roofs, lanterns and good coffee all within 200 m.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> Shops ≈11:00–22:00<br>• Lunch options are right here (Changhwadang is in Ikseon).<br>• Narrow lanes: go before 12:00 to beat the weekend crowd.<br>Naver: 익선동 한옥거리]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9897,37.5723,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 06 Nov 13:00 · Changhwadang Ikseon (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Lunch<br><b>Korean · dumplings &amp; tteokbokki in a hanok</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩12–16k per person, food only<br>In the 1920s hanok lanes of Ikseon-dong: crisp pan-fried dumplings, cheese tteokbokki and cold noodles, at long wooden tables under a glass roof.<br><i>The lanes themselves — go before 12:30 or queue.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None; waitlist app at the door.<br><b>Hours:</b> 11:30–21:30<br>• Two dumpling plates + one tteokbokki between you ≈₩28k total.<br>• Dessert at Cheongsudang's garden café next door.<br>Naver: 창화당 익선동]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9902,37.5738,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 06 Nov 13:00 · Woo Lae Oak — cold noodles only (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Lunch<br><b>Korean · Pyongyang naengmyeon</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩17–20k per person, food only<br>If you skipped it on Thursday: Seoul's legendary 1946 naengmyeon house, 12 minutes' walk south.<br><i>The definitive Pyongyang cold noodles.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> No reservations.<br><b>Hours:</b> 11:30–21:30 · <b>Closed:</b> Mondays<br>• Budget tip: naengmyeon only (≈₩17k); the bulgogi is ₩50k+.<br>Naver: 우래옥 본점]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.998,37.5672,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 06 Nov 14:30 · Rest at the hotel (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Afternoon<br><b>Rest</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>Pack, nap, rooftop. The last big day deserves a pause.<br><i>Zero commute.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> —<br>Naver: 글루호텔]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0039779,37.5758065,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 06 Nov 14:30 · Last gifts in Insadong (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Afternoon<br><b>Shopping</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free to wander<br>Ten minutes' walk: hanji paper, ceramics, tea and the Ssamziegil craft arcade for the final presents.<br><i>Tax refund at the counter over ₩15k.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈10:30–20:30<br>• Kyung-in Museum of Fine Art has a hanok tea garden for a break.<br>Naver: 인사동길]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9855,37.574,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 06 Nov 16:00 · Jongmyo Shrine — English guided tour (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Late afternoon<br><b>Price:</b> ₩1,000<br>The Confucian shrine to the Joseon kings — a 100 m-long hall of dark wood and stone, UNESCO-listed, and the quietest world-heritage site in downtown Seoul.<br><b>Hours:</b> Weekdays by guided tour only · English tours 10:00 / 12:00 / 14:00 / 16:00 · <b>Closed:</b> Tuesdays<br>• Be at the ticket gate by 15:50 — the 16:00 English tour is the last.<br>• Saturdays are free-roaming; weekdays guided only.<br>Naver: 종묘]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.994,37.5745,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 06 Nov 16:00 · Cheonggyecheon stream walk (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Late afternoon<br><b>Riverside walk</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>Drop below street level and follow the restored stream west from Gwangjang towards Gwangtonggyo: stepping stones, bridges, and lanterns switching on as the light goes.<br><i>A calm 2 km of water through the middle of the city — perfect before a farewell dinner.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> Always open<br>• Walk west: the lanterns are best between Gwanggyo and Cheonggye Plaza.<br>• Jongsamyook BBQ is a 10-min walk back east.<br>Naver: 청계천]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.978,37.5695,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 06 Nov 18:30 · Jongsamyook (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Farewell dinner: Korean barbecue<br><b>Korean BBQ · aged pork &amp; beef</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩25–35k per person, food only (≈150–200 g pp)<br>A polished grill house by Jongno 3-ga where the staff cook thick-cut aged pork and beef over charcoal for you, with kimchi stew to finish.<br><i>Staff-grilled, so you can just hold hands and eat.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> Reserve a 18:30 table via Catch Table or Naver a few days ahead.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈16:00–23:00<br>• Budget tip: 300–400 g between two plus one stew stays under ₩35k each.<br>• Drinks extra: soju ≈₩5k, beer ≈₩5k.<br>Naver: 종삼육 종로3가]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9912,37.5711,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 06 Nov 18:30 · Hando Samgyeopsal (food option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-food</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Farewell dinner: Korean barbecue<br><b>Korean BBQ · pork belly sets</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩18–25k per person, food only (sets ≈₩13–14k per portion)<br>The old-school neighbourhood grill: thick pork belly on a slanted iron plate with kimchi and bean sprouts frying in the fat, and a bowl of doenjang stew.<br><i>Cheap, loud and exactly what a last night in Seoul should taste like.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> Walk in; busy after 19:00.<br><b>Hours:</b> ≈15:00–23:00<br>• Two portions (≈₩27k) + rice + stew is plenty for two.<br>• Order 'mokssal' (neck) too if you want variety.<br>Naver: 한도삼겹살 종로]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9906,37.5716,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 06 Nov 20:30 · Namsan night view from Baekbeom Square (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Last night in Seoul<br><b>Night view</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>The lawn terraces on Namsan's north slope with the tower lit above you and the whole downtown glittering below — where you started eleven nights ago.<br><i>Bring a convenience-store drink; the fortress wall is lit along the path.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h<br>• Taxi ≈12 min from Jongno 3-ga (≈₩8k).<br>• Taxi back to the hotel ≈15 min.<br>Naver: 남산공원 백범광장]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.98,37.5547,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 06 Nov 20:30 · Gwanghwamun Square by night (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Last night in Seoul<br><b>Night walk</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>The great axis of Seoul lit at night: King Sejong and Admiral Yi, the floodlit Gwanghwamun gate, and the fountains along the square.<br><i>Fifteen minutes' walk from the barbecue; benches and the media wall.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h<br>• Walk down Cheonggyecheon on the way back.<br>• Taxi home ≈10 min.<br>Naver: 광화문광장]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9768,37.5725,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fri 06 Nov 20:30 · Rooftop at glue hotel (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> Last night in Seoul<br><b>Rest · rooftop</b><br><b>Price:</b> Free<br>Naksan's wall lit above the hotel, the last bags half-packed, and nowhere else to be.<br><i>Zero commute; the lounge set is on until 22:00.</i><br><b>Booking:</b> None.<br><b>Hours:</b> —<br>Naver: 글루호텔]]></description><Point><coordinates>127.0039779,37.5758065,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sat 07 Nov — · Airport bus 6011 from the Changgyeonggung stop (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> To the airport<br><b>Price:</b> Bus ≈₩17–18k pp · taxi ≈₩65–80k<br>Incheon International Airport — the way home. Hold hands in the queue.<br><b>Hours:</b> Bus every 15–20 min from ≈05:00<br>• Check your terminal — T1 or T2.<br>• Tax-refund counters are after security; keep the receipts handy.<br>Naver: 인천국제공항]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9723,37.5559,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sat 07 Nov — · Taxi to Incheon (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> To the airport<br><b>Taxi · ≈60–75 min</b><br><b>Price:</b> ≈₩65–80k per car (+ ₩6.6k toll)<br>Door to door with the bags, ≈60–75 min outside rush hour via the airport expressway; Kakao T shows the fixed estimate before you book.<br><i>Zero hassle for two people with luggage.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> 24 h<br>• Say which terminal — Terminal 1 for most airlines, 2 for KAL/Delta/Air France/KLM.<br>• Book via Kakao T to avoid negotiating.<br>Naver: 인천국제공항]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.4407,37.4602,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sat 07 Nov — · AREX express from Seoul Station (option)</name><styleUrl>#Seoul-opt</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<b>Choice:</b> To the airport<br><b>Train · 43 min · ₩11k</b><br><b>Price:</b> ₩11,000 per person + taxi ≈₩10k<br>Taxi 15 min to Seoul Station, then the non-stop AREX Express to Incheon T1 in 43 min (T2 51 min); some airlines allow check-in at Seoul Station.<br><i>Traffic-proof and you can drop bags early at the station check-in counters.</i><br><b>Hours:</b> 05:20–22:40, every 25–40 min<br>• Check if your airline offers city check-in at Seoul Station (KAL, Asiana, Jeju Air).<br>• Buy express tickets at the machines — the all-stop train takes 20 min longer.<br>Naver: 서울역 공항철도]]></description><Point><coordinates>126.9707,37.5547,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
</Document></kml>